    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`build_claude` is now implemented in `app/claude.py`. I tested it with a stub transport that fails the first request with a 500 and succeeds on the retry, and all three egress rules held. `pytest -q` passes (1 test), and `fetch_status` is unchanged.\n\n**One thing to know first:** the pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` sends requests through `httpx2`, not `httpx`. It rejects `httpx` clients and timeouts, so I couldn't pass it the client from `build_http_client()` or `app/http.py`'s `TIMEOUT`. Instead, `build_claude` makes an `httpx2` client using the SDK's `DefaultHttpxClient`, built from what `app/http.py` already defines:\n\n- `X-Egress-Tag: EGRESS_TAG` as a client header.\n- `log_egress` as the request hook, so every attempt is logged once, retries included.\n- A new `httpx2.Timeout(READ_TIMEOUT, connect=CONNECT_TIMEOUT)`, which gives a 5 s connect and 60 s read timeout. It's set on both the HTTP client and the `Anthropic` client, because the SDK applies its own timeout to each request.\n- `max_retries=2`.\n\nWhen `transport` is given, the client sends through it. When it's `None`, the argument is left out entirely so the SDK keeps its default transport, which has TCP keepalive.\n\n**Stub check results:** 2 attempts, each tagged `ops-assistant`. The \"app.egress\" log had exactly 2 lines (`POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages`), and the request timeout was connect 5.0 / read 60.0.\n\n**For your tests:** the stub transport must be an `httpx2` one, such as `httpx2.MockTransport`. An `httpx.MockTransport` will be rejected by the SDK.\n\n**Cleanup:** removing my scratch script `check_claude.py` from the project root needed approval, so it's still there. Please delete it.\n\nI relied on the bundled Claude API docs and the installed SDK source, and didn't query Context7."
  },
